Creation of Splunk Enterprise

Step 1 — Create Splunk Account & Download
Go to: https://www.splunk.com
Sign up (free account required)

Download:
Splunk Enterprise (Linux .deb) for Ubuntu

Step 2 — Install Splunk on Ubuntu

Move to your download directory:
cd ~/Downloads

Install the package:
sudo dpkg -i splunk-*.deb

If dependencies fail:
sudo apt-get install -f -y

Step 3 — Start Splunk
sudo /opt/splunk/bin/splunk start
First run will:

Ask you to accept license → type Y
Ask you to create:

Username:
Password:
Save these credentials

Step 4 — Enable Auto Start (optional)
sudo /opt/splunk/bin/splunk enable boot-start

Step 5 — Access Splunk Web UI

Open browser:
http://<your-server-ip>:8000
Example:
http://192.168.1.100:8000

Step 6 — Login
Use:
Username: (you created)
Password: (you created)

Step 7 — Convert to Free License
By default, you’re on a trial.
To switch to free:

Go to:
Settings → Licensing
Click:
Change License Group
Select:
Free

Restart Splunk:
sudo /opt/splunk/bin/splunk restart

Step 8 — Add Data (Important)
Option A: Monitor local logs

In UI:
Settings → Add Data

Choose:
Files & Directories

Add:
/var/log/syslog

Option B: Install Universal Forwarder (Windows)
On your Windows lab machine:
Download Splunk Universal Forwarder
Install it

During setup:
Enter Splunk server IP
Port: 9997 (we’ll enable this next)

Step 9 — Enable Receiving Port
On Splunk server:
Go to:
Settings → Forwarding and receiving

Click:
Configure receiving
Add port:
9997

Step 10 — Search Logs

Go to:
Search & Reporting

Try:
index=_internal

Or:
index=* | head 20

Step 11 — Test with Real Activity

On your system:
whoami
ls

Then search:
index=* whoami

LIMITATIONS
❌ No authentication features (some RBAC disabled)
❌ Max 500 MB/day ingestion
❌ No clustering
